COPY={}
def page(route,title,eyebrow,intro,sections=(),subtitle=''):
 COPY[route]={'title':title,'eyebrow':eyebrow,'intro':intro.strip(),'sections':sections,'subtitle':subtitle}
page('index.html','Hi, I’m Sarah.','SARAH SAFWAN / DIGITAL MARKETING & CREATIVE WORK','''I started making things long before I knew what to call any of it professionally. As a teenager, I was already interested in writing, researching subjects that caught my attention, and making little projects feel like something bigger than an idea sitting in my notes. I didn’t have a detailed career plan. I just kept finding things I wanted to create and learning whatever I needed to make them happen.

That curiosity became Thy Academia, an independent platform I founded and ran for three years. I wrote and researched articles, developed visual content, worked out how to present complicated subjects in accessible ways, and handled the practical reality of keeping an independent project moving. Later, I created a podcast, continued writing, explored short-form content, and started working more directly with brands. Each project required a different set of decisions, and together they helped me discover what kind of work I genuinely enjoy.

These days, my focus is digital marketing and creative work: the research behind an idea, the angle that makes it interesting, the way it should look and sound, and the decisions that turn it into something usable. I enjoy the early stages when you’re still figuring out what the work should be, but I also care about the less glamorous parts: reviewing details, correcting inconsistencies, improving an edit, and making sure the finished result actually serves its purpose.

This portfolio brings together professional work, independent projects, and ideas I’ve developed because I was curious enough to follow them through. They’re not all the same kind of work. That’s part of the point.''',[
('Different projects. Different ways of thinking.','''I don’t think every project needs to be explained with the same three buzzwords. A jewelry brand collaboration involves very different creative decisions from an educational publishing platform, and a community strategy concept asks different questions from fashion campaign production. The selections below show those differences rather than flattening them into a list of services.'''),
('I learned by starting things.','''My work did not begin with a job title. It began with taking responsibility for projects that required me to figure things out as I went. Founding Thy Academia meant writing the content, deciding how to organize it, designing accompanying graphics, and keeping the whole platform coherent. Producing The Sae Talk brought in another kind of discipline: turning a topic into something that could be spoken about and listened to. Independent writing made me much more attentive to voice, structure, and whether an argument actually says something.

Working with brands added another layer. A personal idea can keep changing until it feels right to you; a brand project also has an audience, a brief, a visual identity, deliverables, and other people’s expectations. I started paying closer attention to the questions underneath a piece of content: Who is it for? What should somebody understand, feel, or do after seeing it? Is the visual idea actually achievable? Does it fit the brand without becoming interchangeable with everything else online?'''),
('What I can help you make.','''Some clients need a complete set of social content. Others already have footage and need it edited properly. Some have a product but aren’t sure which creative angle will help it stand out. Others need website copy that sounds human, a presentation that makes sense, or a website that organizes their work clearly. I offer defined packages for recurring needs and individual services for projects that don’t fit a preset scope. The starting prices give an idea of scale; the actual quote follows the work involved.'''),
('Have something in mind?','''You can come to me with a finished brief or an idea that’s still taking shape. Tell me what you’re working on, what you need produced, and where you’d like it to go. I’ll take a look and we can work out whether it’s a good fit.''')])
page('work.html','Work, with the story behind it.','SELECTED WORK','''I wanted this section to show more than finished visuals. A finished piece tells you what somebody produced, but not necessarily how they approached it, what they had to consider, or the choices that made one format more appropriate than another. That’s why the case studies go beyond thumbnails and service labels.

The projects here span brand collaborations, marketing research, campaign-related creative production, and independent publishing. Some were developed as part of a professional role; others grew out of ideas I pursued independently. I describe the work in the context in which it happened, including my actual role, the format, and the particular decisions I worked through.

You can browse by discipline, or simply open the projects that interest you. The most revealing details are usually inside.''')
page('case-bellabelles-egypt.html','BellaBelles Egypt','BRAND COLLABORATION / SOCIAL CONTENT / 2026','''When you’re creating content for a jewelry brand, it’s easy to rely on the same familiar formula: a close-up of the piece, a pretty background, and a line about how much someone needs it. I wanted the work I made for BellaBelles to explore more than one way of introducing the products. Sometimes the jewelry could be part of a personality. Sometimes it could be the punchline to a relatable idea. And sometimes the strongest choice was to let the details of the product do the talking.

I took part in the BellaBelles Egypt Creators Program in August and September 2026, developing a collection of social-first pieces across static design, a five-slide carousel, short-form video, and animation. These formats weren’t interchangeable. Each had a different pace, visual purpose, and relationship to the products. The work required both creative concepting and the practical production decisions needed to make each piece feel consistent, legible, and suitable for social media.''',[
('Which BellaBelles Girl Are You?','''The starting point for this carousel was a simple observation: people don’t always choose accessories because they match a trend. They also choose pieces because something about them feels familiar. A shape, color, or mood can remind you of the version of yourself you like dressing as. I built the carousel around that idea rather than presenting a conventional product lineup.

The five slides introduce three distinct personalities. The Seaside Sweetheart is paired with Blush Pony, The Autumn Muse with Autumn Cascade, and The Golden Girl with The Whale. Rather than describing these as three products to buy, the carousel gives the audience three moods to recognize themselves in. The closing slide asks which BellaBelles girl they are, turning the product presentation into a question that invites a response.

The sequencing matters. The cover introduces the premise before showing any personality result. The three middle slides share enough visual consistency to feel like one series, while allowing the individual names and pieces to stand apart. The final slide brings the reader back into the concept rather than ending with a generic shopping command. It works as a carousel because each swipe advances the same idea.'''),
('Fixing Pain as an Adult','''This piece takes a familiar childhood solution and contrasts it with the way an adult might reach for a little treat instead. The joke is deliberately uncomplicated: sometimes what looks like a small indulgence is exactly what someone wants after a difficult day. Jewelry becomes the grown-up version of a quick fix, without pretending to offer anything deeper than a playful moment.

The format is important here. Unlike the personality carousel, this idea needs to be understood in one glance. The copy and imagery share the work of delivering the joke, so neither should overpower the other. The point wasn’t to make an elaborate campaign out of a single observation; it was to create a standalone piece with a recognizable, social-media-friendly premise.'''),
('Seahorse earrings styling video','''The seahorse earrings video takes a much closer view of the product. The interest comes from shape, movement, how the earrings sit when styled, and the small details that are easier to appreciate in motion than in a still image. Its job is not to carry a long story. It’s to make the product visually interesting enough to keep watching for a few seconds.'''),
('Autumn-leaf earrings styling video','''The autumn-leaf earrings required a different visual character. The leaf shape is already expressive, so the styling and close-ups focus on helping that shape read clearly. Even when two videos share the same basic format, they shouldn’t feel like copies with different products inserted. The jewelry itself should influence the footage selected, the rhythm, and what detail gets the viewer’s attention.'''),
('Jewelry Wishlist animation','''The Jewelry Wishlist animation turns a familiar feeling into a moving graphic: seeing pieces you like and mentally adding them to an ever-growing list. Unlike the styling videos, it isn’t built around physical product movement. It works through graphic pacing, sequencing, and a playful visual announcement. Including it alongside the videos shows the difference between editing moving footage and building motion into a designed composition.'''),
('What connects the pieces','''The collaboration gave me the opportunity to approach the same product category through several formats without forcing all the content into one concept. The personality carousel asks the audience to identify with a mood. The static design relies on a joke. The product videos let the jewelry be the subject. The animation brings an idea to life through movement and typography. Across all of them, the larger task was to find an approach appropriate for the product and format, then make deliberate choices about how it was presented.''')],subtitle='One jewelry brand, several ways to tell its story.')
page('case-moving-truck.html','DAXUEN: Developing the visual idea','PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE / FASHION MARKETING / 2026','''My work at DAXUEN has given me a more practical understanding of creative development within a fashion-marketing context. An idea can sound exciting in a conversation and still fall apart when you ask what the camera should see, how the scene works spatially, or why one shot should follow the next. A significant part of the process has involved pushing concepts beyond the initial description and examining whether the visual result actually matches the intended direction.

The experience has brought together creative ideation, campaign thinking, AI-assisted image and video development, and detailed review. In a streetwear context, styling, attitude, movement, environment, and framing all contribute to what the viewer understands about the brand. A compelling image doesn’t come from a collection of fashionable keywords. It requires a coherent scene, recognizable styling, and a clear point of view.''',[
('How I approach a visual brief','''I begin by identifying the element that needs to remain consistent and the element that makes each shot interesting. For a fashion concept, that often means preserving a model’s appearance and outfit while changing the setting, camera angle, or action. A useful brief has to make those priorities explicit. Otherwise, a new image might be visually impressive but no longer represent the same character or product.

My work has involved translating creative direction into precise instructions: what should be visible, what should be happening, how the subject relates to the environment, and what must not change. I also consider the practical logic of a set. A believable image needs consistent reflections, plausible room geometry, correctly placed objects, and movement that makes sense. Those details are easy to overlook until a generation or edit gets them wrong.'''),
('What happens after the first result','''The first output is rarely the end of the process. Reviewing the work means looking past whether it is immediately eye-catching and asking more specific questions. Does the styling match the brief? Are hands, accessories, and reflections consistent? Does a background feel like a real location? Is the model behaving naturally? Has a creative effect made the scene more interesting, or simply made it harder to understand?

When working with moving-image concepts, continuity becomes particularly important. The position of a camera, the physical direction of movement, and the transition from one action to another can determine whether a sequence feels intentional. My role has included refining those instructions and reviewing results with that continuity in mind.'''),
('Creative work inside a larger marketing role','''This experience has also made me think about how an individual visual fits into the wider content plan. A campaign doesn’t only consist of its most ambitious asset. It also needs supporting formats, different ways of presenting a product, and enough variation to keep the story interesting without losing its identity. Developing campaign-related content has therefore involved thinking about imagery, video, social posts, and short supporting formats as connected parts of a larger communication effort.

What I value most from the work is the habit of being specific. Having ideas matters, but so does being able to explain them clearly, recognize when something isn’t working, and make the revisions that bring the execution closer to the concept.''')],subtitle='A concept is only useful if it can be made to work.')
page('case-advertising-research.html','DAXUEN: Reading the competition','MARKETING RESEARCH / COMPETITIVE ANALYSIS / 2026','''Part of my marketing work with DAXUEN involved studying how other streetwear brands present themselves through paid advertising. That meant going beyond a general impression of a brand’s aesthetic and looking at the actual creative material it was putting in front of audiences: the kinds of products featured, the formats used, the message emphasized, and the role of styling, imagery, and promotional language.

The research focused on five competitors: Jaded London, Aelfric Eden, The Ragged Priest, mnml, and NAMED collective. I examined their publicly available advertising using Google Ads Transparency Center and Meta Ads Library, approaching each platform as a different window into the brands’ communication choices.''',[
('What I wanted to understand','''An ad can be visually striking without telling you very much about the strategy behind it. I was interested in comparing patterns. Which brands leaned on product-focused creative, and which relied more on an overall fashion identity? How prominently did they use promotional messaging? How did the clothing itself appear on camera? Were they showing products plainly, building a lifestyle around them, or using particular creative formats to make the brand recognizable?

Rather than reducing each competitor to a generic strength or weakness, I organized the research brand by brand. This gave the work enough structure to compare approaches without assuming every company should advertise in exactly the same way.'''),
('Turning observations into useful research','''A useful competitor review needs to separate what is observable from what is inferred. Seeing a particular creative approach in an advertising library does not tell you the company’s performance or revenue. It does, however, provide evidence of what that brand is choosing to communicate publicly. I used those visible examples as the basis for describing each advertiser’s approach and discussing possible lessons relevant to DAXUEN.

The work required organizing references, making comparisons between formats and creative styles, and summarizing findings so they could be discussed without losing the important details. I also considered what might be applicable to DAXUEN’s own content direction rather than treating competitors’ work as a template to copy.'''),
('What this work shows','''Creative research isn’t separate from creative development. When done carefully, it helps you understand a category before making decisions within it. This project strengthened my ability to review real advertising examples, recognize repeated creative choices, distinguish evidence from opinion, and communicate observations in a way that can inform a marketing conversation.''')],subtitle='Looking at advertising with a question in mind.')
page('case-thy-academia.html','Thy Academia','FOUNDER / INDEPENDENT PUBLISHING / 2022–2025','''I founded Thy Academia in 2022, when I was already interested in research, education, writing, and the everyday difficulties that surround student life. I wanted a place where those interests could become accessible content rather than remain scattered ideas. Over the following three years, I developed it into an independent publishing project built around educational subjects, studying, and student wellbeing.

Because it was my own project, I couldn’t treat writing, design, planning, and publishing as separate departments. If a topic needed research, I did that work. If an article needed a clearer structure, I had to edit it. If a subject needed a visual explanation or a social post, I needed to decide what that should look like. Running the platform meant paying attention to the content itself and the practical questions that determined whether it could be published consistently.''',[
('Finding subjects worth writing about','''Educational writing has a particular challenge: it can become either too vague to teach anything or so dense that people stop reading. I worked on finding a better middle ground. The objective was to make subjects about studying, personal development, and mental wellbeing understandable without treating the reader as someone who needed to be talked down to.

That involved researching topics, choosing angles, deciding how to introduce a subject, and organizing the material into a piece with a clear reason for existing. I became attentive to the difference between an interesting subject and an article that actually communicates something. A topic alone isn’t enough; you need to decide what the reader should understand by the time they finish.'''),
('One idea, more than one format','''The platform also involved adapting content for different contexts. An article can make room for explanations and careful transitions; an Instagram post needs a more immediate entry point and a visual structure that can carry part of the meaning. Working across Medium and social content required me to think about those differences rather than simply cut an article into shorter sentences.

I developed graphics, social posts, and accompanying content while managing the overall presentation of Thy Academia. The work taught me to think about consistency in a practical way: not repeating the same layout or phrase, but making different pieces feel like they belong to the same project.'''),
('Learning to manage the whole thing','''Founding a project is exciting when you’re coming up with ideas. Maintaining one requires a different kind of attention. I had to work through content planning, research, drafting, revisions, visual preparation, and publication without the structure of a larger team. Some of the most useful lessons were ordinary ones: a good idea still needs a realistic plan, a draft usually improves when you return to it, and taking responsibility for every stage gives you a much clearer understanding of how the stages affect each other.

Thy Academia ended in 2025, but it remains one of the clearest examples of my independent work. It was where I began learning how to turn personal curiosity into something organized, public, and sustained over time. A lot of what I do now in marketing and creative production still draws on that experience.''')],subtitle='The project that taught me how much goes into making something of your own.')
page('case-the-sae-talk.html','The Sae Talk','INDEPENDENT PODCAST / COMPLETED PROJECT','''The Sae Talk was an independent podcast I created and hosted, beginning in 2024. It grew from my interest in conversations about personal experiences, perspective, and the things people often struggle to put into words. Writing had already given me a way to explore those subjects, but audio changed how I had to approach them. A written paragraph can be reread. In a podcast, the listener follows the thought as it unfolds, and the way you speak becomes part of the meaning.

I developed the podcast from its concept through to published episodes. My responsibilities included deciding on subjects, planning episode structure, preparing scripts or talking points, recording, editing, and thinking about how the show should be presented. It was both a creative project and an exercise in managing a production process that was different from the written and visual work I had done before.''',[
('Finding the right structure for a conversation','''I wanted episodes to feel conversational rather than like someone reading an essay aloud. That required deciding what belonged in the introduction, which examples made a topic relatable, where a shift in the discussion felt natural, and when a point had been explored enough. There is a balance between sounding spontaneous and knowing where you’re going. Too much scripting makes the episode stiff; too little structure can leave the listener wondering what the point was.

The editing process brought another layer of decision-making. Spoken material has hesitations, repetitions, changes of pace, and moments that work differently when you listen back. Reviewing recordings taught me to pay attention not only to what I meant to say, but to what the listener would actually hear.'''),
('What the project added to my work','''Producing The Sae Talk expanded my understanding of storytelling beyond the page. It required an awareness of tone, pacing, audience attention, and the relationship between preparation and a natural voice. Those are skills I continue to use when working with scripts, short-form video, and other content intended to sound human rather than overly rehearsed.

The Sae Talk is now a completed project. It remains part of my portfolio because it documents a meaningful period of independent audio production and the work involved in bringing a show from idea to release.''')],subtitle='Some subjects make more sense when you can hear someone think them through.')
page('case-substack.html','Writing things I actually want to say.','INDEPENDENT WRITING / EDITORIAL WORK','''My Substack is the part of my work where I don’t have to begin with a product, a brand brief, or a format chosen for an algorithm. I can begin with a question, an observation, or a feeling that doesn’t seem quite settled, and give it the space to develop. I write about personal experiences, emotions, growth, and the way ordinary things can take on more meaning than we expect them to.

What matters to me is finding the right perspective, not simply choosing a topic that sounds reflective. A piece needs a reason to exist. Sometimes that means taking apart a familiar idea and asking whether I really agree with it. Sometimes it means writing about a feeling that I can recognize but haven’t yet worked out how to explain. In either case, I want the writing to reach an actual thought rather than circle around a polished-sounding sentence.''',[
('How I work on a piece','''I often begin with fragments: a line, a question, a disagreement, a memory, or something I noticed. From there, the work is to find out what connects them. I develop the structure as the argument becomes clearer, then revise sentences that feel too vague, overly dramatic, or unlike something I’d actually say. Being personal isn’t the same thing as being imprecise. The more specific the observation, the more likely it is to resonate with somebody else.

Editing is a significant part of the process. I look for places where a paragraph repeats an earlier point, where an example doesn’t really support the idea, or where a neat conclusion is trying to finish a thought that isn’t finished yet. Sometimes the best choice is to leave a little uncertainty in the piece rather than force an answer.'''),
('Writing as part of a wider creative practice','''Independent writing is not the same work as marketing copy, and I don’t want to present it as if the two are interchangeable. But writing regularly has made me more sensitive to tone, audience, and whether words communicate something specific. I carry that attention into website writing, concept development, and editing, even when the format and objective are entirely different.''')])
page('experience.html','How the work developed.','EXPERIENCE','''My experience combines professional brand work, independent creative projects, and research-based writing. Those categories have given me different responsibilities. A marketing internship means developing ideas and research within a broader team’s work. A brand creators program involves interpreting a product and delivering content against a defined brief. Building an independent platform means making and managing most of the decisions yourself. I value the differences because each taught me something I couldn’t have learned from the others alone.''',[
('DAXUEN | Marketing Intern','''At DAXUEN, I’ve worked across several parts of fashion marketing rather than one isolated production task. My experience includes campaign concept development, content planning, AI-assisted image and video work, influencer-marketing exposure, competitor research, and advertising analysis. The creative work has required turning broad ideas into directions specific enough to produce and review. The research side has involved examining public advertising from comparable streetwear brands and organizing findings into useful observations. Together, those responsibilities have helped me understand how research, creative execution, and campaign decisions influence one another.'''),
('BellaBelles Egypt | Creators Program · 2026','''The BellaBelles Creators Program gave me the opportunity to develop a small body of social-first work for a jewelry brand, rather than one repeated type of product post. I worked on concepting, graphics, a personality-led carousel, short-form styling videos, and animation. Each deliverable called for a different treatment: character and audience identification for the carousel, humor for the static design, product detail for the videos, and pacing for the animated wishlist. It was an opportunity to practice adapting a creative idea to the product and platform instead of relying on a single visual formula.'''),
('Thy Academia | Founder · 2022–2025','''I founded and ran Thy Academia as an educational publishing project centered on student life, studying, and wellbeing. My responsibilities covered subject research, writing, editing, graphic design, editorial planning, social content, and the ongoing management of the platform. Working without a separate team meant learning how every stage connected, from deciding whether a topic was worth covering to preparing the finished piece for publication. Thy Academia was one of my earliest sustained experiences of taking full responsibility for a creative project.'''),
('The Sae Talk | Creator and Host · Completed','''I created and hosted The Sae Talk as an independent audio project. Developing episodes required finding meaningful subjects, preparing a conversational structure, recording, editing, and preparing the show for release. Podcasting helped me understand storytelling in a format where pacing and tone are heard rather than read. The project has ended, but it remains an important part of my experience with editorial development and digital production.'''),
('Independent Content Creation | 2024–2026','''Before moving my focus toward behind-the-scenes marketing and client production, I explored creating and editing content independently. That work gave me hands-on experience with the practical realities of short-form media: finding an angle, filming, selecting footage, editing for pace, and adjusting a piece for the platform where it would appear. It also helped me understand what kinds of creative work I wanted to pursue professionally. I now apply those skills primarily to brand and client projects rather than personal creator publishing.'''),
('Life Sculptor | Research Volunteer · 2022–2023','''My earlier volunteer work involved researching psychology-related subjects and working with information intended to help people understand a topic more clearly. It strengthened habits that became useful in later editorial and marketing work: approaching a question carefully, reading beyond the surface, and considering how information needs to be organized for an audience. It forms part of the research foundation behind my writing and content interests.'''),
('Sarah Safwan | Independent Writing','''Alongside other projects, I maintain a practice of long-form writing. It involves developing subjects from observations and experiences, working through structure, revising tone, and making sure a piece earns its conclusion. Writing has been a continuing part of my work even as my professional focus has moved more directly toward marketing and creative production.''')])
page('writing.html','Writing has always been part of the work.','WRITING & AUDIO','''Before I had a clear professional direction, writing was one of the ways I tried to understand what interested me. Sometimes it meant researching a subject and explaining it in an article. Sometimes it meant trying to describe something personal without making it sound bigger or simpler than it was. Later, audio added another layer: learning how to organize thoughts for a listener rather than a reader.

The three projects in this section represent distinct approaches. Thy Academia was educational and editorial, built around planning content for an audience and sustaining an independent publication. The Sae Talk took some of that interest in communication into recorded conversations and episodes. My Substack is a more personal space for long-form pieces in my own voice. They overlap in the importance of language, but they don’t serve the same purpose, and I don’t want to pretend they do.''',[
('Thy Academia','''Educational writing and publishing across student life and wellbeing, supported by visual content and editorial planning. A project I founded and managed across three years.'''),
('The Sae Talk','''An independent, completed podcast exploring personal subjects through scripted preparation, spoken storytelling, recording, and editing.'''),
('Substack','''Long-form reflective writing developed from questions, observations, and experiences I wanted to examine in more depth than a social caption allows.'''),
('Read or listen','''Explore the original work through Medium, Spotify, Apple Podcasts, and Substack. Each link leads to the project as it was actually published.''')])
page('services.html','Creative work, with a clear purpose.','SERVICES','''Not every project needs the same kind of support. Some brands have their direction figured out and need someone to produce the content. Others have plenty of ideas but need help deciding which ones are worth pursuing. Sometimes the problem is the way something is written, edited, organized, or presented. My services cover those different stages, from research and concept development through to design, writing, video editing, presentations, and websites.

I prefer to begin with the job the work needs to do. A carousel intended to explain a product needs a different structure from one made to entertain. A video assembled from existing footage needs clear editorial decisions even if nothing new is filmed. A website has to make information easy to find and understand, not simply look attractive in a screenshot. The package catalogue provides structured starting points, and individual or custom scopes can be discussed when the work calls for something more specific.''',[
('Social media content design','''Static posts, stories, and multi-slide designs planned for how people actually encounter content on a phone. I consider the point of the post, which information needs prominence, and how the design carries the reader through it. The aim is to make a finished piece that belongs to the brand while remaining clear at social-media size.'''),
('Carousel and post production','''A useful carousel has to give someone a reason to swipe and a reason to stay. I work with structure, copy placement, visual consistency, hierarchy, and the relationship between consecutive slides rather than treating them as a set of unrelated graphics.'''),
('Short-form video editing','''Editing provided footage and assets into purposeful short-form pieces, with attention to the first seconds, selection and sequence of shots, pacing, sound, transitions, and the way a video needs to work on its intended platform.'''),
('Content planning','''Developing content directions and calendars that account for what a brand needs to say, what it can realistically produce, and how different post types contribute to a coherent presence. Planning should make production easier, not create an impressive-looking document nobody can use.'''),
('Creative concept development','''Finding the idea behind the execution: researching references, developing angles, working through how a concept could appear across formats, and making the direction clear enough for actual production.'''),
('Marketing research','''Competitor, content, and advertising research intended to support choices. The focus is on identifiable patterns and useful observations, with a distinction between what the examples show and what might be inferred from them.'''),
('Writing and copy','''Website copy, social copy, and editorial material that takes audience, tone, structure, and intended use seriously. The right wording depends on context; the goal is clarity and character rather than an endlessly repeated brand voice template.'''),
('Brand visuals and presentations','''Visual directions, style references, layouts, and presentations organized to help an idea communicate clearly. These services can support a defined creative need without pretending every assignment requires a complete identity overhaul.'''),
('Websites and updates','''Designing and organizing responsive website experiences that make it easy to understand what someone offers, browse their work, and get in touch. Scope can range from a focused landing page to a larger content-driven site, with maintenance and additional pages considered separately.'''),
('UGC production','''Creating on-camera or faceless brand content delivered directly for use on a client’s own channels. Production, revisions, usage licensing, advertising rights, and additional footage are scoped separately as applicable.'''),
('How a project moves forward','''You tell me what you’re working on and what you need from me. I review the scope, materials, timeline, and intended use, then we agree on the deliverables and terms. From there, production follows the brief, feedback is consolidated at the agreed review points, and the final files are delivered in the formats needed. Nothing about the process needs to be mysterious, but it should be specific enough that both sides know what to expect.''')])
page('calculator.html','Find the right starting point.','SERVICES / PACKAGES','''The packages are here to make common requests easier to understand and compare. Each service has three levels of scope, so you can see what changes when the deliverables become more substantial. Prices are shown as starting from amounts rather than promises that every project will cost the same regardless of its requirements.

You can choose International, Egypt, or Lebanon to view the agreed package rates for your market. Some services are straightforward to scope from a deliverable count. Others depend on technical requirements, existing assets, timelines, or the amount of development involved, so their final quotation needs a closer look. Individual services, additional deliverables, and combinations outside the listed packages are available on request.''',[
('Intro to service tiers','''Choose the service first, then compare the three options. Each card explains what is included and provides a way to enquire about that specific package.'''),
('Something a little different?','''Not every project arrives neatly packaged. If you need one deliverable, an unusual combination of services, or something that requires a more tailored approach, send me the details. I’ll review what the project needs and prepare a quote around the actual scope.''')])
page('contact.html','Tell me what you’re working on.','CONTACT','''I’m open to discussing freelance projects, creative collaborations, and professional marketing opportunities. If you have a brief, feel free to send it. If the project is still at the idea stage, that’s fine too; a little context about what you want to achieve is enough to start a conversation.

For creative work, it helps to know what the project is about, which deliverables you have in mind, whether you already have materials to work from, and the timeframe you’re considering. For a role, send the position and a sense of the responsibilities involved. I’ll respond based on the scope and my availability.''')
page('case-rhode.html','From Influence to Involvement','INDEPENDENT STRATEGY / INTERACTIVE WEBSITE CONCEPT','',[
('The idea behind the project','''Rhode already has a strong identity. Its products, visual style, and founder-led presence give people a clear sense of what world the brand belongs to. I wasn’t interested in proposing another campaign to make the brand more visible. I wanted to explore what the relationship could look like after somebody already knew the brand and had begun using its products.

That led me to a question: what would happen if a beauty brand gave its everyday customers a more active part in the conversation? Not a one-off comment box or a campaign hashtag, but an ongoing place where someone could ask a question, share an opinion about a product, suggest a change, and see what happened afterward.'''),
('Moving from a question to an experience','''I developed the proposal around a verified customer community integrated into Rhode’s website. Its features are connected intentionally. Membership establishes access. Discussions create an ongoing space for honest product conversations. Suggestions and voting make it easier to surface recurring requests. Testing opportunities offer a structured way to gather more detailed experiences. Founder Q&A sessions introduce a more direct kind of conversation. An impact timeline closes the loop by showing what happens to customer input after it’s collected.

I didn’t want the concept to exist only as a presentation. I built an interactive prototype so someone could navigate the proposed experience and understand how its parts relate to one another. Designing the interface made the strategic questions more concrete: what does a user see first, what motivates them to return, where does a contribution go, and how do you make feedback visible without turning it into an empty promise?'''),
('What I paid attention to','''The most important challenge was making the experience feel like a plausible extension of Rhode rather than a separate, generic social network with a beauty logo added. The interface therefore draws on the brand’s current clean, product-led visual language, while organizing community activity into clear, useful sections. I also considered how the concept could create room for criticism as well as enthusiasm. If a brand only wants praise, it doesn’t need a community; it needs another promotional feed.

The full case study goes into the membership model, founder access, feedback process, product co-creation, experiential possibilities, and the rationale for measuring participation. The strategy PDF explains the proposal in depth; the interactive website lets visitors try the flow themselves.''')])
